<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ndlela kaSompisi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1840">1840</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ndlela</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ndlela kaSompisi was a general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulu-kingdom">Zulu</a> kings Shaka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dingane">Dingane</a>, who died in February 1840. Of non-Zulu, Ntuli origin, he distinguished himself in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndwandwe-zulu-war">Ndwandwe–Zulu War</a> and rose quickly under Shaka, who was said to promote soldiers on merit whatever their origin. Under Dingane he was the highest political and military officer of the kingdom: inDuna or chief adviser, prime minister and commander-in-chief of the army. He commanded at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blood-river">Battle of Blood River</a> in 1838, a significant Zulu defeat by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andries-pretorius">Andries Pretorius</a>. He refused to kill Dingane's half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mpande">Mpande</a>, and after defeat at Maqongqo in 1840 Dingane had him executed by strangulation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ndlela kaSompisi</b>, a general of Shaka and Dingane, was Dingane’s chief adviser; Ntshingwayo kaMahole commanded the army that won at Isandlwana.</blockquote><a href="/societies/zulu-kingdom">Social Hierarchy of the Zulu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
